@prefix agp: <https://dagalog.no/ns/agentprov#> .
@prefix bl: <https://dagalog.no/ns/backlog#> .
@prefix ghissues: <https://github.com/daghovland/rdf-datalog/issues/> .
@prefix ghpull: <https://github.com/daghovland/rdf-datalog/pull/> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix session: <https://dagalog.no/ns/agentprov/session#> .

# PROSPECTIVE summary scaffold, generated by scripts/new-provenance-summary.sh.
# TODO: replace the placeholder summaryText/decisionPoint below with the
# actual distilled reasoning behind this PR before committing — see
# docs/plans/TRANSCRIPT_SUMMARY_GUIDELINES.md. Timestamps below are real
# (from `git log main..HEAD` on branch test/527-query-timeout-http-test); everything else that
# isn't marked TODO can be left as-is.

ghpull:543 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "sparql_endpoint: add HTTP-layer test coverage for the query-timeout 503 response" ;
    bl:number 543 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:527 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr543 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:527 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-13T22:23:27+02:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-17T21:31:33Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr543Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #527 identified that PR #423's Deadline-cancellation timeout mapping in sparql_endpoint/src/query.rs (query_execution_error_response, a string-content match on Deadline::check's exact error message to distinguish 503 from a generic 500) had no HTTP-layer test: TestServer's helpers all hardcoded max_query_timeout_secs to 10, so nothing exercised the real /sparql route with a short enough timeout to trip it. Followed the precedent in sparql_endpoint/tests/request_timeout.rs (#367) for the sibling request_timeout_secs config, adding a matching start_writable_with_query_timeout constructor. The main design decision was how to make a query deterministically slow against a 1-second budget (the field is whole seconds, so 1s is the shortest usable value) without flaking on faster or slower CI machines. sparql_parser/tests/query_timeout_tests.rs sidesteps this by using a 1ms timeout that is 'already elapsed' before evaluation starts; that trick doesn't work for a real 1s wall-clock deadline, so an actually-slow query was needed instead. Benchmarked a transitive-closure property-path query (ex:p+) over synthetic chains of increasing length directly against sparql_parser::execute_with_base (bypassing the HTTP layer for fast iteration) on this session's memory-constrained (3.7GB) environment: 100k nodes took ~1.3s, 200k took ~4.3s, 300k took ~5.7s with no timeout configured, comfortably clearing 1s even accounting for a much faster CI runner. Also tried a large Cartesian-product BGP (two unbound triple patterns joined with no shared variable) as an alternative slow-query shape, but even a few thousand rows per side caused the debug-mode test process to be OOM-killed on this constrained machine, so it was rejected in favor of the chain/property-path shape, which stays memory-light (linear in chain length) while still being CPU-heavy enough to blow well past a 1s budget. Landed on a 300,000-node chain for the timeout-triggering test, run 3x locally with no flakiness observed, plus a small fast-query counterpart under the same short timeout to guard against the slow-query assertion passing only because every query 503s." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:543 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr543 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr543Summary agp:decisionPoint session:pr543Decision .

session:pr543Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Chose a transitive-closure property-path query (ex:n0 ex:p+ ?x) over a 300,000-node linear chain as the deterministically-slow query for tripping a 1s HTTP-layer timeout, sized from direct benchmarking against sparql_parser::execute_with_base outside the HTTP layer (300k nodes: ~5.7s with no timeout, on a 3.7GB-RAM constrained environment)." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered a large Cartesian-product BGP (two independent unbound triple patterns joined with no shared variable, following sparql_parser/tests/query_timeout_tests.rs's cartesian_bgp_respects_timeout pattern) instead of a chain/property-path query. Rejected it because even a few thousand rows per side caused the debug-mode benchmark process to be OOM-killed on this session's constrained machine — the materialized join output grows quadratically and is memory-heavy, whereas the chain/property-path shape's output and working set stay linear in chain length while its BFS work is still large enough to comfortably exceed a 1s budget." .
